    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "Supreme People's Court    Supreme People's Procuratorate\nMinistry of Public Security    Research Office of the Ministry of Labour and Personnel\nReply on How the Legal Provisions Should Be Applied in Revoking the Original Order of Commutation of Sentence\nWhere, after a Life Prisoner's Sentence Has Been Commuted, the Trial Court Finds the Original Judgment\nto Be Indeed Erroneous and Substitutes a New Judgment\n(7 November 1994)\nHigher People's Court of Jiangxi Province:\nYour court's Gan Gao Fa No. 110 [1994] \"Request for Instructions on How the Legal Provisions Should Be Applied in Revoking an Order of Commutation of Sentence\" has been received. After study, the reply is as follows:\nWhere a criminal sentenced to life imprisonment has had his sentence commuted by a ruling made in accordance with law by the higher people's court of the place where he is serving his sentence, and the people's court that originally tried the case finds that the original judgment is indeed erroneous and, in accordance with the trial supervision procedure, substitutes a judgment of fixed-term imprisonment, the original ruling on commutation of sentence shall be revoked in accordance with Fa (Yan) Fu No. 2 [1989] of this Court. In view of the fact that the original ruling on commutation was a commutation on the basis of life imprisonment, and since the original sentence of life imprisonment has been determined to be a wrongful conviction, the original ruling on commutation shall likewise be deemed to be indeed erroneous in respect of the findings of fact and the application of law. Accordingly, it is appropriate for the higher people's court of the place where the criminal is serving his sentence to revoke the original ruling on commutation in accordance with the trial supervision procedure pursuant to the provisions of paragraph 1 of Article 149 of the Criminal Procedure Law.\nAttachment:\nHigher People's Court of Jiangxi Province\nRequest for Instructions on How the Legal Provisions Should Be Applied\nin Revoking an Order of Commutation of Sentence\n21 September 1994      Gan Gao Fa No. 110 [1994]\nSupreme People's Court:\nThis Court previously requested instructions from your court, by the request for instructions and report Gan Fa Yan Er Zi No. 3 (88), on the question of \"whether the original ruling on commutation of sentence should be revoked where a life prisoner's sentence has been commuted and the judgment is subsequently replaced\", and on 3 January 1989 your court gave a reply to that question by Fa (Yan) Fu No. 8 [1989]. In the course of implementing the above reply, we hold different understandings as to how the legal provisions should be applied in revoking an ruling on commutation of sentence in such circumstances. One view is that where, after a criminal sentenced to life imprisonment has had his sentence commuted by a ruling made in accordance with law, it is found that the original judgment is indeed erroneous and the sentence is replaced in accordance with law with fixed-term imprisonment, the original ruling on commutation shall be revoked. The reason for revoking the ruling on commutation is that the basis of the original commutation no longer exists, which may be regarded as an erroneous finding of fact in the original ruling on commutation. Therefore, the written ruling revoking the commutation shall apply the provisions of Article 149 of the Criminal Procedure Law. Another view is that where an ruling on commutation is revoked because the original sentence of life imprisonment was indeed erroneous and has been replaced with fixed-term imprisonment, it is not the original commutation that is erroneous. The ruling on commutation made by the judicial organ on the basis of the criminal's conduct while serving his sentence is not itself erroneous; the reason why the ruling on commutation is to be revoked is that the original sentence has already been replaced and the ruling on commutation has no practical significance, but the revocation of an ruling on commutation does not fall within the scope of application of the trial supervision procedure, and it is unnecessary to cite Article 149 of the Criminal Procedure Law in the written ruling revoking the commutation; at present the Criminal Procedure Law makes no provision for the revocation of an ruling on commutation in such circumstances, and provision should be made when the law is amended in the future.\nWe are not certain how the legal provisions should be applied in revoking an ruling on commutation of sentence, and therefore request instructions from your court. We look forward to your reply."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1."
        }
      }
    ]
